<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Simplifying using laws of logarithms</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Use the laws of logarithms to simplify each expression to a whole number. Do not use a calculator.</p>
<p><strong>(a)</strong> \(\log_{{@b@}} {@m1@} + \log_{{@b@}} {@n1@} - \log_{{@b@}} {@r@}\)</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p><strong>(b)</strong> \({@p@}\log_{{@B@}} {@c@} + \log_{{@B@}} {@d@} - {@j@}\)</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>The laws are: product \(\log m + \log n = \log(mn)\), quotient \(\log m - \log n = \log\dfrac{m}{n}\), power \(p\log m = \log(m^p)\), and \(\log_b b^k = k\).</p>
<p><strong>(a)</strong> Use the product law, then the quotient law:</p>
\[ \log_{{@b@}} {@m1@} + \log_{{@b@}} {@n1@} - \log_{{@b@}} {@r@} = \log_{{@b@}} \frac{{@m1@} \times {@n1@}}{{@r@}} = \log_{{@b@}} {@b^k@} = {@k@}. \]
<p><strong>(b)</strong> Use the power law first, then the product law:</p>
\[ {@p@}\log_{{@B@}} {@c@} + \log_{{@B@}} {@d@} = \log_{{@B@}} {@c^p@} + \log_{{@B@}} {@d@} = \log_{{@B@}} ({@c^p@} \times {@d@}) = \log_{{@B@}} {@B^p@} = {@p@}, \]
<p>so the expression is \({@p@} - {@j@} = {@ta2@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2026063000</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text><![CDATA[b: rand([2,3,5,10]);
k: rand([2,3]);
u: rand(sublist(listify(divisors(b^k)), lambda([t], t > 1 and t < b^k)));
r: rand([3,7]);
m1: u*r;
n1: b^k/u;
ta1: k;
B: rand([6,10]);
c: rand(sublist(listify(divisors(B)), lambda([t], t > 1 and t < B)));
p: rand([2,3]);
d: (B/c)^p;
j: rand([1,2]);
ta2: p - j;]]></text>
    </questionvariables>
